== 10. e4 ==
White takes up the full center. If Black simply plays '''[[/10...O-O/]]''' we are once again following the main line.

One thing that sets this position apart from [[Chess_Opening_Theory/1._d4/1...d5/2._c4/2...c6/3._Nf3/3...Nf6/4._Nc3/4...dxc4/5._a4/5...Bf5/6._e3/6...e6/7._Bxc4/7...Bb4/8._O-O/8...O-O/9._Qe2/9...Bg6|8...O-O 9. Qe2 9...Bg6]] is that White was actually able to play e4. Black can take the pawn but it is not free. '''10...Bxc3''' removes an important defender of e4. After 11. bxc3 Nxe4 12. Ba3! {{chesspunc|compensation}}Black is up a pawn, but White has prevented them from castling king-side. Black will have to castle queen-side, where White has the open b-file and the far-advanced a-pawn.

If Black gets greedy and tries to win more pawns with 12...Nxc3? White is quick to get the upper hand after 13. Qb2!, forking the knight and the pawn on b7.

