==5...cxb5==
After White’s queenside exchanges, it has become clear that the black queenside is hanging on by a thread. Black’s a-pawn cannot truly defend b5 due to the pin by the white rook along the a-file. Now’s the time for White to wreck Black’s queenside. Greco recommended 6. b3, targeting the c4 pawn. After 6...cxb3? 7. Bxb5+ Bd7 8. Qxb3, White regains the pawn while having a massive lead in development. However, Black can simply return the pawn on their own terms and develop, equalising against this archaic move.

The best and most popular way to challenge Black, however, is with 6. Nc3. This attacks the b5 pawn, and takes advantage of the difficulty of defending that pawn.

