==6. Nc3==
Black’s greed had come to bite them back! After 6. Nc3, Black’s b-pawn is attacked and White has plenty of tactical opportunities on the queenside, not least because Black’s a-pawn is pinned by the White rook on a1. From here, Black has three choices here. 

*6...Bd7 - From the 1620s up until the end of the 20th century, this was traditionally regarded as the only way to go. 6...Bd7 manages to defend the b5 pawn, and hold Black’s queenside together, and for the moment keeps the extra pawn. However, White simply develops with 7. Nf3 and maintains their strong centre, taking advantage of how much time Black is wasting. 7. d5 is possibly a stronger alternative, aggressively invading Black’s territory in an aggressive attempt to punish Black’s greed.
*6...a6 - At first glance, this is a textbook mistake that fails to hold on to Black’s extra pawn. However, this move flies in the face of traditional chess wisdom anyway, giving up the a1 rook in an exchange sacrifice. In return however, Black’s development would be accelerated to Mach speed. White will have to tread carefully.
*6..Qb6 - This modern move is the theoretical mainline. Black hangs on to the b5 pawn for the moment. If White responds with 7. b3, Black will return the pawn to develop actively. But wait, doesn’t this lose to 7. Nd5, attacking the queen and eyeing a fork on c7? This ambitious reply by White is common, but this a1 rook, as with the 6...a6 line, comes with strings attached. 

