== 2. Qd3 · Alapin variation ==
A rarer line. White develops their queen aggressively to an active square and attacks the vulnerable f5 pawn.

'''[[Chess Opening Theory/1. d4/1...f5/2. Qd3/2...e6|2...e6]]''' is the most common way of defending the pawn and the second most common move. Black goes into a Stonewall setup and makes the queen seem misplaced. However, the unique line that can arise from 2. Qd3 is 3. g4!?, the tricky '''Manhattan gambit'''.

'''[[Chess Opening Theory/1. d4/1...f5/2. Qd3/2...Nf6|2...Nf6!?]]''' is the most popular move, the so-called '''Pretzel gambit'''<ref>{{Cite web |title=Dutch Defense: Alapin Variation, Pretzel Gambit - Chess Openings |url=https://www.chess.com/openings/Dutch-Defense-Alapin-Variation-Pretzel-Gambit |access-date=2026-05-31 |website=Chess.com |language=en-US}}</ref>. Black ignores that their pawn is hanging and hopes that kicking the queen around after 3. Qxf5 d5 is sufficient compensation.
