== 3...g6 · Leningrad variation ==
Black has entered the '''Leningrad variation''' of the Dutch defence. This move prepares a kingside fianchetto, where Black can then castle their king to safety. Black's ideas are quite similar to the [[Chess Opening Theory/1. d4/1...Nf6/2. c4/2...g6/3. Nc3/3...Bg7|King's Indian defence]], often playing for ...d6 to prepare ...e5 and to attack on the kingside. The main advantage this system has over the King's Indian is that ...f5 is already played, so White wasn't able to build a big centre and Black doesn't have to move their knight on f6 to play ...f5.<ref>{{cite AV media |url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=p0jl7Jxq8rg|access-date=21 October 2026|people=[https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arturs_Neik%C5%A1%C4%81ns Arthur Neikšāns]|last=Neikšāns|first=Arthur|language=English|title=The Leningrad Dutch {{pipe}} GM Neiksans Boot Camp #9}}</ref> 

The main line continues '''[[/4. Nf3/]]''', with the idea of quickly castling kingside. The vast majority of games continue 4...Bg7 5. O-O O-O, which is the main tabia of this variation.

'''[[/4. c4/]]''' is also played, but usually transposes to one of the main lines after 4...Bg7 5. Nc3 O-O 6. Nf3.

'''[[/4. Nh3/]]''' is a sideline, with the idea of getting the knight to f4 and starting a kingside attack with h2-h4-h5. This transfer of the knight can also occur in other lines where White postpones this knight's development.

