== 2. h3 · Korchnoi attack ==
The Korchnoi attack is sideline against the Dutch defence. Although it looks irregular for White to play a pawn move in the opening that doesn't help their development, White's intention is to support 3. g4, attacking the f5-pawn to regain control of the centre. This is a similar idea to [[../2. g4|2. g4?!]], the Krejcik gambit, but with an improved execution. 

The two most common continuations are 2...Nf6 and 2...d5. 

[[/2...Nf6|'''2...Nf6''']] is the typical second move for Black in the Dutch defence. Here it adds another attacker to g4, so 3. g4 is a gambit called the Janzen-Korchnoi gambit. 3...fxg4?! 4. hxg4 Nxg4 and White can now play 5. e4 with a discovered attack on the knight and compensation for the pawn. 5. Qd3 instead sets up a trap: White attacks h7 twice, and after 5...Nf6?? 6. Rxh7! Rxh7?? 7. Qg6#. 

[[/2...d5|'''2...d5''']] simultaneously adds a defender to f5 (the bishop) and adds control over e4 (the pawn). After 3. g4?! fxg4 4. hxg4 Bxg4, Black has won a pawn and e4 is still out of reach for White, as the e4 square is controlled and the e-pawn pinned to the queen. 

=== History ===
[[Image:Viktor Kortsjnoj, Bestanddeelnr 930-3470.jpg|thumb|right|Korchnoi at the European Chess Cup in 1979.]] 
[[w:Carl Mayet|Carl Mayet]] (1810―1861) played 2. h3 in his 1839 match series against [[w:Baron von der Lasa|Baron von der Lasa]].<ref>[https://www.chessgames.com/perl/chessgame?gid=1266146 Mayet vs. von der Lasa, 1839. Chessgames.com]</ref> 

[[w:Viktor Korchnoi|Viktor Korchnoi]] (1931―2016) uncorked this gambit at the Swiss Championship in Biel, 1979.<ref>[https://www.chessgames.com/perl/chessgame?gid=1082507 Korchnoi vs Kaenel, 1979. Chessgames.com]</ref> Korchnoi, known as one of the strongest players never to become world champion, dominated the competition but was not crowned Swiss champion as he was not yet a Swiss citizen. He received political asylum in Switzerland in the same year.<ref>{{Cite news |title=Korchnoi a obtenu l'asile politique |date=1979-09-25 |page=27 |work=FAN L'Express |url=https://www.e-newspaperarchives.ch/?a=d&d=EXR19790925-01.2.171 |access-date=2026-10-08 |language=French}}</ref>

