== 3. Bd3 · Mieses Attack ==
3. Bd3 develops a bishop and prepares to recapture on e4. It is mainly used as a surprise weapon to take Black out of familiar Caro–Kann theory.

Black has one main response: '''3...dxe4,''' fighting for equality, with '''3...e5''' being the only other move that does not hand white a clear advantage.

The main idea of the Mieses Attack is to take black out of theory and to place the bishop on f3 to control the d1-h5 diagonal in order to prepare a g4 push in the near future.

After '''3...dxe4 4. Bxe4 Nf6 5. Bf3''' Black has several options:
* '''5...Bf5''' is met by '''6 .g4''', a brave move, known as the Hibbins-Khan Variation, hitting the loose bishop on f5. An alternative is '''6. Ne2''', which is more tame.
* '''5...Nbd7''' prepares an early ...e5, breaking open the centre.
* '''5...g6''' is one of the critical tries for equality, preparing to fianchetto the bishop.
'''3...e5''' is a reasonable alternative that is very unlikely to be seen, as it is an unnatural move in an already very rare opening. The idea is that black is now attacking both the d4 and e4 pawns. After '''4. dxe5 dxe4 5. Bxe4 Qxd1+ 6. Kxd1''' black is left in a playable pawn down queenless middlegame.

===History===
The opening was first played by Franz Hölzl in 1979. Notably it has been played by Indian grandmaster Aravindh Chitambaram against Raunak Sadhwani in 2019, where Aravindh won in a chaotic battle. The name Mieses Attack comes from Grandmaster Jacques Mieses 

