=4. c3 · Caro-Kann - Botvinnik-Carls Defence =

White’s most popular response to the Botvinnik-Carls Defence according to the Lichess database, accounting for more than half of white’s moves in the preceding position.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Caro-Kann Defense: Advance Variation, Botvinnik-Carls Defense |url=https://lichess.org/opening/Caro-Kann_Defense_Advance_Variation_Botvinnik-Carls_Defense/e4_c6_d4_d5_e5_c5 |access-date=2024-08-09 |website=lichess.org |language=en-US}}</ref> While the move is neither the engine’s top choice, nor that of most grandmasters, it still leaves white with a small advantage. The objective of the move is to strengthen the center and enable a recapture with the pawn in the event of [[Chess Opening Theory/1. e4/1...c6/2. d4/2...d5/3. e5/3...c5/4. c3/4...cxd4|4...cxd4]]. It is best for black to put more pressure on the d4 pawn before taking the pawn, which can best be done with the move [[Chess Opening Theory/1. e4/1...c6/2. d4/2...d5/3. e5/3...c5/4. c3/4...Nc6|4...Nc6]].  

