|moves=1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 Qf6 4. c3 a6 5. Ba4 Bc5 6. d4 exd4
|caption=A: White has the intermezzo 7. e5! where 7...Nxe5? 8. Qe2!.
}}
{{Chess/board
|moves=1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 Qf6 4. O-O a6 5. Ba4 Bc5 6. c3 Nge7 7. d4 exd4
|caption=B: White can't win back d4 because ...Nge7 "closed" the e-file. 7. e5? Nxe5!. 
}}
</div>

'''4. O-O''' is the other most natural move in the Spanish. In this case it may be a small inaccuracy, because the time it gives Black to develop makes it harder to achieve c3 and d4. Compare 4. O-O a6 5. Ba4 Bc5 6. c3 Nge7 7. d4 exd4 (diagram B) to the position in diagram A: the intermezzo 8. e5? fails to ...Nxe5, because Qe2 no longer pins the knight. 

'''4. Nc3''' is a less obvious move to play in the Spanish, because it interferes with the plan of c3 and d4. However, it makes the most direct attempt to "punish" Black's unprincipled play by threatening 5. Nd5, which both displaces Black's queen from support of the centre and threatens Nxc7+.

If 4...a6 5. Nd5! Qd8, while White has won tempo on the queen, they have not in fact won the pawn:

* Though Black has had to undefend e5, White cannot win the pawn as 6. Bxc6? dxc6! comes with tempo on the knight. After 7. Nc3 (transposing to the [[Chess Opening Theory/1. e4/1...e5/2. Nf3/2...Nc6/3. Bb5/3...a6/4. Bxc6/4...dxc6/5. Nc3|Keres variation of the exchange Spanish]]) Black has time to defend e5 again with 7...f6 or 7...Qd6. 
* Similarly, as is often the way, 6. Ba4 Nf6 does not threaten to win White's "undefended" e-pawn either, due to tactics involving Black's uncastled king. (e.g. 7. c3 Nxe4? 8. d4 exd4? 9. Qe2! etc.)

Instead, the role of 4. a6 5. Nd5! is more one of latent pressure that Black cannot remove easily. The engine line is 6. Ba4 Nf6 7. c3 Nxd5 8. exd5 e4! 9. dxc6! exf3 10. cxd7+ Bxd7 11. Bxd7+ Qxd7 12. Qxf3{{chess/not|++}}, where Black gives up a pawn to remove it.

Aman Hambleton prefers to meet 4. Nc3 with 4...Bd6!? (defending c7 and e5) so that 5. Nd5 can be met with 5...Qg6, attacking g2 and e4. Though the engine is happy to castle and give up the e-pawn (6. O-O Qxe4 7. Nc3 Qg6 8. d4{{chess/not|++}}), the resulting positions are razor-sharp and 6. d3 also works as the g2 pawn is poisonous (of course). 

Best for Black is 4...Nge7, to deny White Nd5 (or to trade knights if they play it anyway). 

=== History ===

3...Qf6 has no official name in the Encyclopaedia of Chess Openings (ECO). The book ''The Ruy Lopez: Winning Chess with 1 P-K4'' (1963) by Leonard Barden, predating the ECO, calls this variation the "Frankfurt Defence".<ref name="barden">{{Cite book |title=The Ruy Lopez: Winning Chess with 1 P-K4 |last=Barden |first=Leonard |publisher=Pergamon Press |year=1963 |isbn=0 08 009997 1 |location=Oxford |url=https://archive.org/details/ruylopezwinningc0000bard|pages=153-4}}</ref> They place it towards the "outrageous" end of their list of "irregular and bizarre defences". Barden doesn't provide an attribution but the name presumably comes from 
Jean Taubenhaus v Louis Paulsen during the 5th DSB Congress, held Frankfurt, Germany in 1887.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Jean Taubenhaus vs Louis Paulsen (1887) |url=https://www.chessgames.com/perl/chessgame?gid=1266423 |access-date=2025-06-28 |website=www.chessgames.com}}</ref>

Documented theory for 3...Qf6 dates back to at least 1843 in ''Handbuch des Schachspiels'' (German: ''Handbook of Chess''). It cites the dubious line 4. c3 a6 5. Bxc6 Qxc6 6. O-O Qxe4 7. Nxe5 Be7 8. Re1 Qf5 9. d4 d6 10. Nf3 Be6. The ''Handbuch'' notes that White is likely better off with 4. Nc3 instead.<ref>{{Cite book |last=Baron von Heydebrand und der Lasa |first=Tassilo |url=https://play.google.com/books/reader?id=BU8rAAAAYAAJ&pg=GBS.PP1&hl=en |title=Handbuch des Schachspiels |publisher=Veit |year=1843 |location=Germany |pages=168 |language=de}}</ref> 

One of the earliest recorded games of 3...Qf6 was played in Howard Staunton vs Bernhard Horwitz at the London Chess Club in 1846.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Howard Staunton vs Bernhard Horwitz (1846) |url=https://www.chessgames.com/perl/chessgame?gid=1271912 |access-date=2025-06-28 |website=www.chessgames.com}}</ref> 

In 2021, Grandmaster Aman Hambleton experimented with this opening and, finding there was no official name for it in the ECO, gave it the eponym "The Hambleton Defence" and noted its tricky king-side play.<ref name="hambleton">{{Cite web |title=I invented a NEW chess opening |url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=UMaawPAAi68 |last=Hambleton |first=Aman (Chessbrah) |date=2021-11-17 |access-date=2025-06-18 |website=Youtube}}</ref>

The [[wikipedia:Chess.com|Chess.com]] opening book refers to the opening as the "Ruy López Opening: Gunderam Variation" apparently by analogy with the Gunderam defence, [[Chess Opening Theory/1. e4/1...e5/2. Nf3/2...Qe7|1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Qe7]]. In the Gunderam defence, Black also defends the e5 pawn with the queen, but this involves an early Qe7 not Qf6 and [[wikipedia:Gerhart Gunderam|Gerhart Gunderam]] is not known to have had an affiliation with 3...Qf6.

