<br>So Schliemann Defence champion Teimour Radjabov prefers the more sedate '''[[Chess Opening Theory/1. e4/1...e5/2. Nf3/2...Nc6/3. Bb5/3...f5/4. Nc3/4...fxe4/5. Nxe4/5...Nf6|5...Nf6]]''', with the character of the resulting positions depending on whether White now exchanges on f6 voluntarily or has to be forced into it with 6.Qe2 d5.{{wikipedia|Ruy Lopez}}{{Chess Opening  Theory/Footer}}

{{Chess  diagram|tright|'''Ruy Lopez, Schliemann  Defence'''|rd||bd|qd|kd|bd||rd|pd|pd|pd|pd|||pd|pd|||nd|||nl||||bl|||pd|||||||||||||||||nl|||pl|pl|pl|pl||pl|pl|pl|rl||bl|ql|kl|||rl|'''Common moves:'''<br> [[/6...Qxf6|6...Qxf6 - Main Line]]<br>  [[/6...gxf6|6...gxf6 - Bokan Variation]]|=}}
[[Chess Opening Theory/1. e4/1...e5/2. Nf3/2...Nc6/3. Bb5/3...f5/4. Nc3/4...fxe4/5. Nxe4/5...Nf6/6. Nxf6/6...Qxf6|'''6...Qxf6''']] is a useful developing move - many writers caution against bringing the queen out early, but without the b1-knight on the board she doesn't have to worry about losing tempos to Nb1-c3-d5 or Nb1-c3-e4 ideas. Any opening that features a quick ...e5 and ...f5 (looking at you, Latvian Gambit) will likely involve putting the queen on f6 and castling kingside to bring a rook to f8 and then mating White on the f-file.

[[Chess Opening Theory/1. e4/1...e5/2. Nf3/2...Nc6/3. Bb5/3...f5/4. Nc3/4...fxe4/5. Nxe4/5...Nf6/6. Nxf6/6...gxf6|'''6...gxf6''']] has been played by FM Danko Bokan. You get the same pawn structure as in the 6.Qe2 d5 7.Nxf6+ line but White still has the option of putting her queen on a square other than e2.{{wikipedia|Ruy Lopez}}{{Chess Opening  Theory/Footer}}

{{Chess  diagram|tright|'''Ruy Lopez, Schliemann  Defence'''|rd||bd||kd|bd||rd|pd|pd|pd|pd|||pd|pd|||nd|||qd||||bl|||pd|||||||||||||||||nl|||pl|pl|pl|pl||pl|pl|pl|rl||bl|ql|kl|||rl|'''Common moves:'''<br> [[/7. Qe2|7. Qe2 - Main Line]]<br> [[/7.  O-O|7. O-O - Kupreichik's Variation]]|=}}
Having avoided committing the queen to e2 on move 6, White typically puts it there anyway with [[Chess Opening Theory/1. e4/1...e5/2. Nf3/2...Nc6/3. Bb5/3...f5/4. Nc3/4...fxe4/5. Nxe4/5...Nf6/6. Nxf6/6...Qxf6/7. Qe2|'''7. Qe2''']]. This restricts Black's options, because now ...d5 would self-pin the c6-knight allowing White to capture the e5-pawn for zero compensation. 

Viktor Kupreichik mixed things up a couple times with [[Chess Opening Theory/1. e4/1...e5/2. Nf3/2...Nc6/3. Bb5/3...f5/4. Nc3/4...fxe4/5. Nxe4/5...Nf6/6. Nxf6/6...Qxf6/7. O-O|'''7. O-O''']]. If Black reacts the same way she would after 7. Qe2, with 7...Be7, White has a huge positive score with the plan of Bxc6 and Qe1. But alert Black players found 7...Nd4! to take advantage of the fact that the e-pawn isn't under any pressure.{{wikipedia|Ruy Lopez}}{{Chess Opening  Theory/Footer}}

{{Chess  diagram|tright|'''Ruy Lopez, Schliemann  Defence'''|rd||bd||kd|bd||rd|pd|pd|pd|pd|||pd|pd|||nd|||qd||||bl|||pd|||||||||||||||||nl|||pl|pl|pl|pl|ql|pl|pl|pl|rl||bl||kl|||rl|'''Common moves:'''<br> [[/7...Be7|7...Be7 - only move]]|=}}
Attempting to defend the e-pawn with moves like 7...d6 or 7...Bd6 are ultimately futile. Either 7...d6 8.d4 or 7...Bd6 8.d4 (not allowing Black the trick 8.Bxc6 dxc6 9.d4 Bg4) just win it outright.

So instead Black gambits the pawn with [[Chess Opening Theory/1. e4/1...e5/2. Nf3/2...Nc6/3. Bb5/3...f5/4. Nc3/4...fxe4/5. Nxe4/5...Nf6/6. Nxf6/6...Qxf6/7. Qe2/7...Be7|'''7...Be7''']]. The compensation will be in the form of open lines for the rooks and an unopposed light-squared bishop.{{wikipedia|Ruy Lopez}}{{Chess Opening  Theory/Footer}}

{{Chess  diagram|tright|'''Ruy Lopez, Schliemann  Defence'''|rd||bd||kd|||rd|pd|pd|pd|pd|bd||pd|pd|||nd|||qd||||bl|||pd|||||||||||||||||nl|||pl|pl|pl|pl|ql|pl|pl|pl|rl||bl||kl|||rl|'''Common moves:'''<br> [[/8. Bxc6|8. Bxc6 - only move]]|=}}
White accepts the gambit with [[Chess Opening Theory/1. e4/1...e5/2. Nf3/2...Nc6/3. Bb5/3...f5/4. Nc3/4...fxe4/5. Nxe4/5...Nf6/6. Nxf6/6...Qxf6/7. Qe2/7...Be7/8. Bxc6|'''8. Bxc6''']], planning to capture on e5 once the guard is removed.

Castling kingside is still sub-optimal due to the ...Nd4 option (compare the position after 6...Qxf6).{{wikipedia|Ruy Lopez}}{{Chess Opening  Theory/Footer}}

{{Chess  diagram|tright|'''Ruy Lopez, Schliemann  Defence'''|rd||bd||kd|||rd|pd|pd|pd|pd|bd||pd|pd|||bl|||qd|||||||pd|||||||||||||||||nl|||pl|pl|pl|pl|ql|pl|pl|pl|rl||bl||kl|||rl|'''Common moves:'''<br> [[/8...bxc6|8...bxc6 - Radjabov  Variation]]<br> [[/8...dxc6|8...dxc6 - Old Main Line]]|=}}
Here's an example of how chess opening theory moves with the times.
* [[Chess Opening Theory/1. e4/1...e5/2. Nf3/2...Nc6/3. Bb5/3...f5/4. Nc3/4...fxe4/5. Nxe4/5...Nf6/6. Nxf6/6...Qxf6/7. Qe2/7...Be7/8. Bxc6/8...dxc6|'''8...dxc6''']] was by far the more common move until the mid-2000s. It seems logical to open a diagonal for the bishop that it can actually use, right? The same exchange in the Exchange Ruy Lopez is always met with dxc6.
* But then along came Teimour Radjabov and revived the whole 5...Nf6 line based on the innovation [[Chess Opening Theory/1. e4/1...e5/2. Nf3/2...Nc6/3. Bb5/3...f5/4. Nc3/4...fxe4/5. Nxe4/5...Nf6/6. Nxf6/6...Qxf6/7. Qe2/7...Be7/8. Bxc6/8...bxc6|'''8...bxc6''']], and now every top player who tries this line - Zvjagintsev, Timmerman, Fressinet - goes 8...bxc6.
Why 8...bxc6 and not 8...dxc6? The intelligent answer is something to do with capturing towards the centre and pawn islands. The honest answer is that Black players tried 8...dxc6 and laid an egg, so they tried something different.{{wikipedia|Ruy Lopez}}{{Chess Opening  Theory/Footer}}

{{Chess  diagram|tright|'''Ruy Lopez, Schliemann  Defence'''|rd||bd||kd|||rd|pd||pd|pd|bd||pd|pd|||pd|||qd|||||||pd|||||||||||||||||nl|||pl|pl|pl|pl|ql|pl|pl|pl|rl||bl||kl|||rl|'''Common moves:'''<br> [[/9. Nxe5|9. Nxe5 - Main Line]]<br>  [[/9. Qxe5|9. Qxe5 - Draw Variation]]<br> [[/9. d4|9. d4 - Berg-Shirov Variation]]}}
* [[Chess Opening Theory/1. e4/1...e5/2. Nf3/2...Nc6/3. Bb5/3...f5/4. Nc3/4...fxe4/5. Nxe4/5...Nf6/6. Nxf6/6...Qxf6/7. Qe2/7...Be7/8. Bxc6/8...bxc6/9. Nxe5|'''9. Nxe5''']] is the main line. White simply captures the free pawn with the nearest piece to hand.
* [[Chess Opening Theory/1. e4/1...e5/2. Nf3/2...Nc6/3. Bb5/3...f5/4. Nc3/4...fxe4/5. Nxe4/5...Nf6/6. Nxf6/6...Qxf6/7. Qe2/7...Be7/8. Bxc6/8...bxc6/9. Qxe5|'''9. Qxe5''']] has been played by Michael Adams, because being a pawn up and taking the queens off is usually what you want to do, but in this case simplifying hurts White's winning percentage as well as Black's. The queen enjoys being the most mobile piece on the board; if you exchange her off then that title would pass to the Black bishops, and there are two of them.
* [[Chess Opening Theory/1. e4/1...e5/2. Nf3/2...Nc6/3. Bb5/3...f5/4. Nc3/4...fxe4/5. Nxe4/5...Nf6/6. Nxf6/6...Qxf6/7. Qe2/7...Be7/8. Bxc6/8...bxc6/9. d4|'''9. d4''']] is a fun idea of Swedish GM Emanuel Berg's, taken up by Alexei Shirov. It's not a gambit in that if Black grabs the pawn with 9...exd4, White shoves the queen away with 10.Bg5 and grabs it back. It's a pseudo-gambit in that White had the chance of going 1 pawn up and instead decided to be 0 pawns up.
{{wikipedia|Ruy Lopez}}{{Chess Opening  Theory/Footer}}

{{Chess  diagram|tright|'''Ruy Lopez, Schliemann  Defence'''|rd||bd||kd|||rd|pd|pd|pd||bd||pd|pd|||pd|||qd|||||||pd|||||||||||||||||nl|||pl|pl|pl|pl|ql|pl|pl|pl|rl||bl||kl|||rl|'''Common moves:'''<br> [[/9. Nxe5|9. Nxe5 - Main Line]]<br>  [[/9. Qxe5|9. Qxe5 - Queen Swap Variation]]|=}}

[[Chess Opening Theory/1. e4/1...e5/2. Nf3/2...Nc6/3. Bb5/3...f5/4. Nc3/4...fxe4/5. Nxe4/5...Nf6/6. Nxf6/6...Qxf6/7. Qe2/7...Be7/8. Bxc6/8...dxc6/9. Nxe5|'''9. Nxe5''']] is the main line. White needs to capture the pawn now otherwise Black will play ...Bd6 and ...Bg4 and the window will be shut.

Why is it not a good idea to capture with [[Chess Opening Theory/1. e4/1...e5/2. Nf3/2...Nc6/3. Bb5/3...f5/4. Nc3/4...fxe4/5. Nxe4/5...Nf6/6. Nxf6/6...Qxf6/7. Qe2/7...Be7/8. Bxc6/8...dxc6/9. Qxe5|'''9. Qxe5''']], since White will be a pawn up with the queens off? Because Black doesn't have to obligingly exchange queens on e5. Instead she can play 9...Bg4 to exploit the brittle knight on f3, which won't have any good squares after 10.Qxf6 Bxf6, and if White has to play gxf3 her kingside pawn majority will be nobbled. (10.Qxc7? is suicide with White so under-developed. The simple 10...O-O! gives Black an irresistible attack.){{wikipedia|Ruy Lopez}}{{Chess Opening  Theory/Footer}}

{{Chess  diagram|tright|'''Ruy Lopez, Schliemann  Defence'''|rd||bd||kd|||rd|pd|pd|pd||bd||pd|pd|||pd|||qd|||||||nl||||||||||||||||||||pl|pl|pl|pl|ql|pl|pl|pl|rl||bl||kl|||rl|'''Common moves:'''<br> [[/9...Bf5|9...Bf5 - the slightly less  committal move]]<br> [[/9...O-O|9...O-O - the slightly more committal  move]]|=}}

[[Chess Opening Theory/1. e4/1...e5/2. Nf3/2...Nc6/3. Bb5/3...f5/4. Nc3/4...fxe4/5. Nxe4/5...Nf6/6. Nxf6/6...Qxf6/7. Qe2/7...Be7/8. Bxc6/8...dxc6/9. Nxe5/9...Bf5|'''9...Bf5''']] is a useful developing move. [[Chess Opening Theory/1. e4/1...e5/2. Nf3/2...Nc6/3. Bb5/3...f5/4. Nc3/4...fxe4/5. Nxe4/5...Nf6/6. Nxf6/6...Qxf6/7. Qe2/7...Be7/8. Bxc6/8...dxc6/9. Nxe5/9...O-O|'''9...O-O''']] is also a useful developing move.

Why is ...Bf5 more popular than ...O-O? It comes down to a general principle of opening theory: postpone the hard decisions for as long as you can. Black in this position no longer has to decide where to put the c8-bishop. The White knight is hitting d7 and g4, and on e6 the bishop will be vulnerable to tactics like Nxc6 as soon as White gets a rook to the e-file. So it has to go to f5. That's an easy decision. Whether Black wants to castle queenside or kingside is a hard decision. Make the easy decision first!{{wikipedia|Ruy Lopez}}{{Chess Opening  Theory/Footer}}

{{Chess  diagram|tright|'''Ruy Lopez, Schliemann  Defence'''|rd||bd|qd|kd|bd||rd|pd|pd|pd|pd|||pd|pd|||nd|||nd||||bl|||pd||||||||nl|||||||||nl|||pl|pl|pl|pl|ql|pl|pl|pl|rl||bl||kl|||rl|'''Common moves:'''<br> [[/6...d5|6...d5 - only move]]|=}}
[[Chess Opening Theory/1. e4/1...e5/2. Nf3/2...Nc6/3. Bb5/3...f5/4. Nc3/4...fxe4/5. Nxe4/5...Nf6/6. Qe2/6...d5|'''6...d5''']] is a beautiful freeing move in most 1.e4 e5 openings, and here Black gets it for free thanks to the awkward knight on e4.

6...Qe7 makes less sense, because if the queen is planning to capture on f6 then it's a waste of a tempo, and if the g-pawn is planning to capture on f6 then the queen isn't needed on e7 to defend the e-pawn because the new f-pawn will be defending it.{{wikipedia|Ruy Lopez}}{{Chess Opening  Theory/Footer}}

{{Chess  diagram|tright|'''Ruy Lopez, Schliemann  Defence'''|rd||bd|qd|kd|bd||rd|pd|pd|pd|||||pd|||nd|||pd||||bl||pd|pd|||||||||||||||||nl|||pl|pl|pl|pl|ql|pl|pl|pl|rl||bl||kl|||rl|'''Common moves:'''<br> [[/8. d4|8. d4 - Main Line]]<br> [[/8.  O-O|8. O-O - Dubai Variation]]<br> [[/8. Nd4|8. Nd4 - Georgiev  Variation]]|=}}
[[Chess Opening Theory/1. e4/1...e5/2. Nf3/2...Nc6/3. Bb5/3...f5/4. Nc3/4...fxe4/5. Nxe4/5...Nf6/6. Qe2/6...d5/7. Nxf6/7...gxf6/8. d4|'''8. d4''']], threatening the e5-pawn, is the move that really gets to grips with Black's pawn centre - given that 8.Nxe5 straight away is not possible because the White queen is pinned after 8...fxe5 9.Qxe5+ Qe7.

Krum Georgiev tried [[Chess Opening Theory/1. e4/1...e5/2. Nf3/2...Nc6/3. Bb5/3...f5/4. Nc3/4...fxe4/5. Nxe4/5...Nf6/6. Qe2/6...d5/7. Nxf6/7...gxf6/8. Nd4|'''8. Nd4''']], not to show off how many Black pieces White has managed to pin, but to threaten a queen check on h5 that would deprive Black of castling rights. It's not such a threat though, as ...Qd6 solidifies the c6-knight and makes room for the king on d8.

An improved version of 8.Nd4 is [[Chess Opening Theory/1. e4/1...e5/2. Nf3/2...Nc6/3. Bb5/3...f5/4. Nc3/4...fxe4/5. Nxe4/5...Nf6/6. Qe2/6...d5/7. Nxf6/7...gxf6/8. O-O|'''8. O-O''']], which was played three times in the 2014 world rapid and blitz championships in Dubai. White postpones the decision about what to put on d4. If Black pre-empts Nd4 with ...Qd6, White can switch tack with 9.d4.{{wikipedia|Ruy Lopez}}{{Chess Opening  Theory/Footer}}

{{Chess  diagram|tright|'''Ruy Lopez, Schliemann  Defence'''|rd||bd|qd|kd|bd||rd|pd|pd|pd|||||pd|||nd|||pd||||bl||pd|pd|||||||pl||||||||||nl|||pl|pl|pl||ql|pl|pl|pl|rl||bl||kl|||rl|'''Common moves:'''<br> [[/8...Bg7|8...Bg7 - only move]]|=}}[[Chess Opening Theory/1. e4/1...e5/2. Nf3/2...Nc6/3. Bb5/3...f5/4. Nc3/4...fxe4/5. Nxe4/5...Nf6/6. Qe2/6...d5/7. Nxf6/7...gxf6/8. d4/8...Bg7|'''8...Bg7''']] is a deceptive move. Yes, it defends the vulnerable e-pawn - but not by attacking the square it's standing on, which wouldn't actually work because it would still have three attackers and two (non-pinned) defenders. Instead it defends the e-pawn indirectly by allowing ...O-O, which will unpin the c6-knight and, more importantly, allow the king's rook to come to the e-file and make the white king and queen uncomfortable.

8...e4 looks impressive but it just freezes Black's central pawns into a block of ice. White can play around the block of ice with Nh4, Bf4 and Qh5, or can play c4 which hits the block of ice with a hammer.

8...Bg4 is also not as powerful as it looks, because 9.dxe5 fxe5 10.Qxe5+ breaks the pin with check, and 9...Bxf3 10.Qxf3 just gives up the bishop pair for nothing. Possibly less than nothing, as it speeds up the ''White'' queen's journey to h5.{{wikipedia|Ruy Lopez}}{{Chess Opening  Theory/Footer}}

{{Chess diagram|tright|'''Ruy Lopez, Schliemann Defence'''|rd||bd|qd|kd|bd|nd|rd|pd|pd|pd||||pd|pd|||nd|||||||bl||pd|pd||||||||nl|||||||||nl|||pl|pl|pl|pl||pl|pl|pl|rl||bl|ql|kl|||rl|'''Common moves:'''<br> [[/6. Nxe5|6. Nxe5 - Main Line]]<br> [[/6. Ng3|6. Ng3 - Showalter Variation]]|=}}

This is a great time to dynamite Black's proud pawn centre with [[Chess Opening Theory/1. e4/1...e5/2. Nf3/2...Nc6/3. Bb5/3...f5/4. Nc3/4...fxe4/5. Nxe4/5...d5/6. Nxe5|'''6. Nxe5''']]! White comes out of the resulting tactics in good shape.

[[Chess Opening Theory/1. e4/1...e5/2. Nf3/2...Nc6/3. Bb5/3...f5/4. Nc3/4...fxe4/5. Nxe4/5...d5/6. Ng3|'''6. Ng3''']] isn't exactly a bad move, and Black still has to do something about the threat to the e-pawn, but who wants to let Black play ...e5 and ...d5 in the first 5 moves and get away with it?{{wikipedia|Ruy_Lopez#Schliemann_Defence:_3...f5}}{{Chess Opening Theory/Footer}}

{{Chess  diagram|tright|'''Ruy Lopez, Schliemann  Defence'''|rd||bd||kd|bd|nd|rd|pd|pd|pd||||pd|pd|||nl|||||||bl||qd|||||||pl||pd||||||||||||pl|pl||pl||pl|pl|pl|rl||bl|ql|kl|||rl|'''Common moves:'''<br> [[/8...Qd6|8...Qd6 - only move]]|=}}

[[Chess Opening Theory/1. e4/1...e5/2. Nf3/2...Nc6/3. Bb5/3...f5/4. Nc3/4...fxe4/5. Nxe4/5...d5/6. Nxe5/6...dxe4/7. Nxc6/7...Qd5/8. c4/8...Qd6|'''8...Qd6''']] is necessary to preserve the attack on the White knight. It's true that Black could have played 7...Qd6 in one move, but the move 8.c4 is hopefully going to prove to be a concession by White.

Horizontal queen moves like 8...Qg5 are inferior because the queen won't be hitting an undefended piece on b5 the way it would after 7...Qg5.{{wikipedia|Ruy Lopez}}{{Chess Opening  Theory/Footer}}

{{Chess  diagram|tright|'''Ruy Lopez, Schliemann  Defence'''|rd||bd||kd|bd|nd|rd|pd|pd|pd||||pd|pd|||nl|qd||||||bl|||||||||pl||pd||||||||||||pl|pl||pl||pl|pl|pl|rl||bl|ql|kl|||rl|'''Common moves:'''<br> [[/9. Nxa7|9. Nxa7+ - Gligoric Attack]]<br>  [[/9. Qh5|9. Qh5+ - Hungarian Attack]]}}

[[Chess Opening Theory/1. e4/1...e5/2. Nf3/2...Nc6/3. Bb5/3...f5/4. Nc3/4...fxe4/5. Nxe4/5...d5/6. Nxe5/6...dxe4/7. Nxc6/7...Qd5/8. c4/8...Qd6/9. Nxa7|'''9. Nxa7+''']] was first played in Gligoric-Heidenfeld 1960. It's not entirely clear how Black is going to punish White for simply grabbing a second pawn and holding on to it - on the contrary, White still has the threat of Qh5+ on the board, so it may be Black that gets the punishment.

[[Chess Opening Theory/1. e4/1...e5/2. Nf3/2...Nc6/3. Bb5/3...f5/4. Nc3/4...fxe4/5. Nxe4/5...d5/6. Nxe5/6...dxe4/7. Nxc6/7...Qd5/8. c4/8...Qd6/9. Qh5|'''9. Qh5+''']] was played against Günther Möhring, the originator of the 7...Qd5 variation, in two Hungarian tournaments in the 1980s. As is usually the case for loud forcing moves, it's actually a little more drawish than the alternative. Black will probably come out of the looming queen trade with castling rights still intact, which isn't the case in the 9. Nxa7+ line.{{wikipedia|Ruy Lopez}}{{Chess Opening  Theory/Footer}}

{{Chess  diagram|tright|'''Ruy Lopez, Schliemann  Defence'''|rd||||kd|bd|nd|rd|nl|pd|pd|bd|||pd|pd||||qd||||||bl|||||||||pl||pd||||||||||||pl|pl||pl||pl|pl|pl|rl||bl|ql|kl|||rl|'''Common moves:'''<br> [[/10. Bxd7|10. Bxd7+ - Move Order A]]<br>  [[/10. Qh5|10. Qh5+ - Move Order B]]}}

White's two threats in this position are Qh5+ and Bxd7+. It doesn't really matter whether White plays
* [[Chess Opening Theory/1. e4/1...e5/2. Nf3/2...Nc6/3. Bb5/3...f5/4. Nc3/4...fxe4/5. Nxe4/5...d5/6. Nxe5/6...dxe4/7. Nxc6/7...Qd5/8. c4/8...Qd6/9. Nxa7/9...Bd7/10. Bxd7|'''10. Bxd7+''']], or
* [[Chess Opening Theory/1. e4/1...e5/2. Nf3/2...Nc6/3. Bb5/3...f5/4. Nc3/4...fxe4/5. Nxe4/5...d5/6. Nxe5/6...dxe4/7. Nxc6/7...Qd5/8. c4/8...Qd6/9. Nxa7/9...Bd7/10. Qh5|'''10. Qh5+''']]
first, unless she wants to leave out Qh5+ entirely and just save the a7-knight with 10.Bxd7 Qxd7 11.Nb5. But that plan has a terrible score for White, despite the two extra pawns, because of Black's bind on the d-file and easy development.{{Chess Opening  Theory/Footer}}

{{Chess  diagram|tright|'''Ruy Lopez, Schliemann  Defence'''|rd||||kd|bd|nd|rd|nl|pd|pd|qd|||pd|pd|||||||||||||||||||pl||pd||||||||||||pl|pl||pl||pl|pl|pl|rl||bl|ql|kl|||rl|'''Common moves:'''<br> [[/11. Qh5|11. Qh5+ - Main Line]]<br>  [[/11. Nb5|11. Nb5 - Retreat Variation]]}}

The main line here is [[Chess Opening Theory/1. e4/1...e5/2. Nf3/2...Nc6/3. Bb5/3...f5/4. Nc3/4...fxe4/5. Nxe4/5...d5/6. Nxe5/6...dxe4/7. Nxc6/7...Qd5/8. c4/8...Qd6/9. Nxa7/9...Bd7/10. Bxd7/10...Qxd7/11. Qh5|'''11. Qh5+''']]. White has the bread and butter of two extra pawns, and wants the jam as well (stopping Black from castling). Black can offer an exchange sacrifice in response, but White shouldn't accept it; the realisation that White isn't obliged to accept it was responsible for 11. Qh5+ taking over as the main line from:

[[Chess Opening Theory/1. e4/1...e5/2. Nf3/2...Nc6/3. Bb5/3...f5/4. Nc3/4...fxe4/5. Nxe4/5...d5/6. Nxe5/6...dxe4/7. Nxc6/7...Qd5/8. c4/8...Qd6/9. Nxa7/9...Bd7/10. Bxd7/10...Qxd7/11. Rb5|'''11. Nb5''']], which seems like it should be enough for a solid advantage. Two pawns are two pawns! But it actually scores rather badly for White. There's a caveat, as there always is with opening statistics: if top players know 11.Qh5+ is playable but weaker players aren't in on the secret, then 11.Nb5 will end up being played by weaker players, and weaker players are more likely to lose.

{{wikipedia|Ruy Lopez}}{{Chess Opening  Theory/Footer}}

{{Chess  diagram|tright|'''Ruy Lopez, Schliemann  Defence'''|rd||||kd|bd|nd|rd|nl|pd|pd|qd|||pd|pd||||||||||||||||ql|||pl||pd||||||||||||pl|pl||pl||pl|pl|pl|rl||bl||kl|||rl|'''Common moves:'''<br> [[/11...g6|11...g6 - only move]]|=}}All of Black's moves in this position look terrible! 11...Qf7?? runs into 12.Qb5+. Moving the king out to e7 also loses at least a pawn to 12.Qe5+. The computer recommends 11...Kd8 as the least bad option, although White could then swing the queen over to a5 threatening Nc6+ and so forcing the king straight back to e8, while potentially saving a tempo by not having to move the knight immediately.

So the modern, active way of defending this position as Black is with [[Chess Opening Theory/1. e4/1...e5/2. Nf3/2...Nc6/3. Bb5/3...f5/4. Nc3/4...fxe4/5. Nxe4/5...d5/6. Nxe5/6...dxe4/7. Nxc6/7...Qd5/8. c4/8...Qd6/9. Nxa7/9...Bd7/10. Bxd7/10...Qxd7/11. Qh5/11...g6|'''11...g6''']]!? It doesn't sacrifice a whole rook, "only" an exchange, because White hasn't got time to capture on h8 and also save the knight.

{{BookCat}}

{{Chess  diagram|tright|'''Ruy Lopez, Schliemann  Defence'''|rd|||||bd|nd|rd||pd||qd||kd||pd|||pd||||pd|||nl|||ql||||||pl||pd||||||||||||pl|pl||pl||pl|pl|pl|rl||bl||kl|||rl|'''Common moves:'''<br> [[/14. Qd4|14. Qd4 - only move]]|=}}

It's now time for White to offer to take the queens off with [[Chess Opening Theory/1. e4/1...e5/2. Nf3/2...Nc6/3. Bb5/3...f5/4. Nc3/4...fxe4/5. Nxe4/5...d5/6. Nxe5/6...dxe4/7. Nxc6/7...Qd5/8. c4/8...Qd6/9. Nxa7/9...Bd7/10. Bxd7/10...Qxd7/11. Qh5/11...g6/12. Qe5/12...Kf7/13. Nb5/13...c6/14. Qd4|'''14. Qd4''']] and hope that the two extra pawns will be sufficiently healthy to win the day.

The retreat 14.Nc3 has been played too, but the attack on e4 is an illusion as long as the e-file is open for the a8-rook (14...Nf6 15.Nxe4? Nxe4 and now 16.Qxe4?? Re8 is a train wreck while 16.Qxh8 Nf6 is just a free piece for Black compared to the earlier Qxh8 lines) so the knight won't be well placed on c3.

{{wikipedia|Ruy Lopez}}{{Chess Opening  Theory/Footer}}

{{Chess  diagram|tright|'''Ruy Lopez, Schliemann  Defence'''|rd||||kd|bd|nd|rd|nl|pd|pd|bd|||pd|pd||||qd||||||bl||||||ql|||pl||pd||||||||||||pl|pl||pl||pl|pl|pl|rl||bl||kl|||rl|'''Common moves:'''<br> [[/10...g6|10...g6 - only move]]|=}}

Black doesn't have an obvious way of escaping the check. 10...Qg6 would allow White with her next two moves Qxg6 and Bxd7 to a) ruin Black's pawns, b) swap off material when ahead and c) prevent Black castling. Fleeing to either d8 or e7 with the king followed by 11.Bxd7 Kxd7 12.Qb5+ would ship yet another pawn, as would 10...Ke7 11.Bxd7 Qxd7 12.Qe5+. The computer recommends 10...Kd8 11.Bxd7 Qxd7 as the least bad option, although White could then swing the queen over to a5 threatening Nc6+ and so forcing the king straight back to e8, while potentially saving a tempo by not having to move the knight immediately.

So the modern, active way of defending this position as Black is with '''[[/10...g6/]]''' followed by 11.Bxd7+ Qxd7!? It doesn't sacrifice a whole rook, "only" an exchange, because White hasn't got time to capture on h8 and also save the knight.{{wikipedia|Ruy Lopez}}{{Chess Opening  Theory/Footer}}

{{Chess  diagram|tright|'''Ruy Lopez, Schliemann  Defence'''|rd||bd||kd|bd|nd|rd|pd|pd|pd||||pd|pd|||nl|qd||||||bl||||||ql|||pl||pd||||||||||||pl|pl||pl||pl|pl|pl|rl||bl||kl|||rl|'''Common moves:'''<br> [[/9...g6|9...g6 - only move]]|=}}

[[Chess Opening Theory/1. e4/1...e5/2. Nf3/2...Nc6/3. Bb5/3...f5/4. Nc3/4...fxe4/5. Nxe4/5...d5/6. Nxe5/6...dxe4/7. Nxc6/7...Qd5/8. c4/8...Qd6/9. Qh5/9...g6|'''9...g6''']] is the only safe move to block the check, because:
* 9...Qg6?? 10.Ne5+ wins the queen directly, and
* 9...Kd7?? 10.Qf7+ and the queen has to be shipped overboard with 10...Qe7, as either 10...Be7 or 10...Ne7 allow the pretty double check 11.Nb8+ followed by mate on e8.
{{wikipedia|Ruy Lopez}}{{Chess Opening  Theory/Footer}}

{{Chess diagram|tright|'''Ruy Lopez, Schliemann Defence'''|rd||bd||kd|bd|nd|rd|pd|pd|pd||||pd|pd|||nl|||||||bl|||||qd||||||pd||||||||||||pl|pl|pl|pl|ql|pl|pl|pl|rl||bl||kl|||rl|'''Common moves:'''<br> [[/8...Nf6|8...Nf6 - only move]]|=}}

Black has to defend the e4-pawn, and does so with [[Chess Opening Theory/1. e4/1...e5/2. Nf3/2...Nc6/3. Bb5/3...f5/4. Nc3/4...fxe4/5. Nxe4/5...d5/6. Nxe5/6...dxe4/7. Nxc6/7...Qg5/8. Qe2/8...Nf6|'''8...Nf6''']], which has the additional benefit of covering h5.

8...Bf5 has a specific tactical problem: 9.f4! is a counter-sacrifice that White wants to play anyway, and with the bishop on f5 the pawn can't be taken: 9...Qxf4?? 10.Rf1 Qg5 11.Rxf5 Qxf5 12.Nd4+ is curtains. Plus, in the current position Black does have a secondary threat of ...a6 and ...Bd7 pinning and winning the c6-knight, which doesn't work if the bishop is away defending e4. Plus, f6 is almost certainly the best square for the knight anyway.

Another line that doesn't work is 8...Qxg2. The downside of playing ...f5 in the opening is the need to be vigilant about queen checks on h5. Here 9.Qh5+ g6 (or 9...Qg6 10.Ne5+) 10.Qe5+ punishes Black severely for her pawn-grabbing. 8...Nf6 cuts out this check, turning the illusory threat of ...Qxg2 into a real one.

{{wikipedia|Ruy_Lopez#Schliemann_Defence:_3...f5}}{{Chess Opening Theory/Footer}}

{{Chess  diagram|tright|'''Ruy Lopez, Schliemann  Defence'''|rd||bd||kd|bd||rd|pd|pd|pd||||pd|pd|||nl|||nd||||bl|||||qd||||||pd||||||||||||pl|pl|pl|pl|ql|pl|pl|pl|rl||bl||kl|||rl|'''Common moves:'''<br> [[/9. f4|9. f4 - Main Line]]<br> [[/9.  Ne5|9. Ne5 - Sax Attack]]<br> [[/9. Nxa7|9. Nxa7 - Svidler Attack]]|=}}

Now Black's attack against g2 is a real one. Defending g2 with moves like 9.0-0 or 9.Rg1 is too slow; Black will have time to execute her secondary threat of ...a6 and ...Bd7. The clever [[Chess Opening Theory/1. e4/1...e5/2. Nf3/2...Nc6/3. Bb5/3...f5/4. Nc3/4...fxe4/5. Nxe4/5...d5/6. Nxe5/6...dxe4/7. Nxc6/7...Qg5/8. Qe2/8...Nf6/9. f4|'''9. f4''']] solves this problem by defending g2 with gain of tempo, while threatening to give the knight a beautiful square on e5 to retreat to, at the cost of restoring material equality (9...Qxf4 is the main reply).    

[[Chess Opening Theory/1. e4/1...e5/2. Nf3/2...Nc6/3. Bb5/3...f5/4. Nc3/4...fxe4/5. Nxe4/5...d5/6. Nxe5/6...dxe4/7. Nxc6/7...Qg5/8. Qe2/8...Nf6/9. Ne5|'''9. Ne5+''']] c6 10.f4 as played by Gyula Sax is just the same moves in a different order.    

But there is an alternative in the controversial [[Chess Opening Theory/1. e4/1...e5/2. Nf3/2...Nc6/3. Bb5/3...f5/4. Nc3/4...fxe4/5. Nxe4/5...d5/6. Nxe5/6...dxe4/7. Nxc6/7...Qg5/8. Qe2/8...Nf6/9. Nxa7|'''9. Nxa7+''']]!? as played by Peter Svidler. It has a very positive score for White, but from a very small sample size. If you can't see what a brilliant move 9.Nxa7+ is, it's probably because <s>the Big 9.f4 industry has hired stooges to infiltrate our message boards and talk it down</s> Magnus Carlsen can't see what a brilliant move 9.Nxa7+ is either.

{{wikipedia|Ruy_Lopez#Schliemann_Defence:_3...f5}}{{Chess Opening  Theory/Footer}}

{{Chess  diagram|tright|'''Ruy Lopez, Schliemann  Defence'''|rd||bd||kd|bd||rd|nl|pd|pd||||pd|pd||||||nd||||bl|||||qd||||||pd||||||||||||pl|pl|pl|pl|ql|pl|pl|pl|rl||bl||kl|||rl|'''Common moves:'''<br> [[/9...Bd7|9...Bd7 - Main Line]]<br>  [[/9...Kd8|9...Kd8 - Leisebein Variation]]|=}}[[Chess Opening Theory/1. e4/1...e5/2. Nf3/2...Nc6/3. Bb5/3...f5/4. Nc3/4...fxe4/5. Nxe4/5...d5/6. Nxe5/6...dxe4/7. Nxc6/7...Qg5/8. Qe2/8...Nf6/9. Nxa7/9...Bd7|'''9...Bd7''']] is the main line. Black preserves castling rights and denies the a7-knight the chance of exchanging itself on c8, which would be the coward's way out. It looks as though the move overworks the f6-knight, but in fact there are tactical reasons why grabbing the e4-pawn doesn't lead to any advantage.

[[Chess Opening Theory/1. e4/1...e5/2. Nf3/2...Nc6/3. Bb5/3...f5/4. Nc3/4...fxe4/5. Nxe4/5...d5/6. Nxe5/6...dxe4/7. Nxc6/7...Qg5/8. Qe2/8...Nf6/9. Nxa7/9...Kd8|'''9...Kd8''']] invites 10.Nxc8 and it looks like White has no problems, but Black has a glimmer of hope based on the fact that g2 is still hanging. German correspondence player Peter Leisebein is the main champion of this line.

9...c6? 10.Nxc6! means the c8-bishop will have to go to d7 anyway, but with one fewer Black pawn on the board.{{wikipedia|Ruy Lopez}}{{Chess Opening  Theory/Footer}}

{{Chess  diagram|tright|'''Ruy Lopez, Schliemann  Defence'''|rd||bd||kd|bd||rd|pd|pd|pd||||pd|pd|||nl|||nd||||bl|||||qd||||||pd|pl|||||||||||pl|pl|pl|pl|ql||pl|pl|rl||bl||kl|||rl|'''Common moves:'''<br> [[/9...Qxf4|9...Qxf4 - Main Line]]<br>  [[/9...Qh4|9...Qh4+ - Czech Variation]]|=}}

[[Chess Opening Theory/1. e4/1...e5/2. Nf3/2...Nc6/3. Bb5/3...f5/4. Nc3/4...fxe4/5. Nxe4/5...d5/6. Nxe5/6...dxe4/7. Nxc6/7...Qg5/8. Qe2/8...Nf6/9. f4/9...Qxf4|'''9...Qxf4''']] is the main line, because why not take a pawn if White's going to leave them lying around.    

[[Chess Opening Theory/1. e4/1...e5/2. Nf3/2...Nc6/3. Bb5/3...f5/4. Nc3/4...fxe4/5. Nxe4/5...d5/6. Nxe5/6...dxe4/7. Nxc6/7...Qg5/8. Qe2/8...Nf6/9. f4/9...Qh4|'''9...Qh4+''']] is similar to the main line, in that the Black queen ends up on h3 via h4 in both lines, except without the bonus of having taken a pawn. A pawn is a pawn!

{{wikipedia|Ruy_Lopez#Schliemann_Defence:_3...f5}}{{Chess Opening  Theory/Footer}}

{{Chess  diagram|tright|'''Ruy Lopez, Schliemann  Defence'''|rd||bd||kd|bd||rd|pd|pd|pd||||pd|pd||||||nd||||bl|||nl||||||||pd|qd|||||||||||pl|pl|pl|pl|ql||pl|pl|rl||bl||kl|||rl|'''Common moves:'''<br> [[/10...c6|10...c6 - only move]]|=}}
Black needs to defend against the check while keeping the knight on f6 to deny the White queen access to h5. 

[[Chess Opening Theory/1. e4/1...e5/2. Nf3/2...Nc6/3. Bb5/3...f5/4. Nc3/4...fxe4/5. Nxe4/5...d5/6. Nxe5/6...dxe4/7. Nxc6/7...Qg5/8. Qe2/8...Nf6/9. f4/9...Qxf4/10. Ne5/10...c6|'''10...c6''']] is a sensible move that leaves two White pieces hanging.

{{wikipedia|Ruy_Lopez#Schliemann_Defence:_3...f5}}{{Chess Opening  Theory/Footer}}

{{Chess  diagram|tright|'''Ruy Lopez, Schliemann  Defence'''|rd||bd||kd|bd||rd|pd|pd|||||pd|pd|||pd|||nd||||bl|||nl|||||||pl|pd||||||||||pl|qd|pl|pl|pl||ql|||pl|rl||bl||kl|||rl|'''Common moves:'''<br> [[/13. Bc4|13. Bc4 - only move]]|=}}
The b5-bishop is finally forced to move, and [[Chess Opening Theory/1. e4/1...e5/2. Nf3/2...Nc6/3. Bb5/3...f5/4. Nc3/4...fxe4/5. Nxe4/5...d5/6. Nxe5/6...dxe4/7. Nxc6/7...Qg5/8. Qe2/8...Nf6/9. f4/9...Qxf4/10. Ne5/10...c6/11. d4/11...Qh4/12. g3/12...Qh3/13. Bc4|'''13. Bc4''']] threatens to revoke Black's castling privileges with a check on f7.

13.Nxc6 would be refuted by the same ...a6 and ...Bd7 trick, pinning and winning the knight, that we saw earlier.{{wikipedia|Ruy_Lopez#Schliemann_Defence:_3...f5}}{{Chess Opening  Theory/Footer}}

{{Chess  diagram|tright|'''Ruy Lopez, Schliemann  Defence'''|rd||bd|qd|kd|bd|nd|rd|pd|pd|pd|pd|||pd|pd|||nd|||||||bl|||pd|pd|||||||pl|||||||pl||nl|||pl|pl|pl|||pl|pl|pl|rl|nl|bl|ql|kl|||rl|'''Common moves:'''<br> [[/4...fxe4|4...fxe4 - only move]]|=}}

The move 4.d3 defended e4 but also threatened to snaffle the f5-pawn into the bargain, because now Black won't have the instant rejoinder ...e4. 

[[Chess Opening Theory/1. e4/1...e5/2. Nf3/2...Nc6/3. Bb5/3...f5/4. d3/4...fxe4|'''4...fxe4''']] is the only move to justify the Black pawn thrust.{{wikipedia|Ruy Lopez}}{{Chess Opening  Theory/Footer}}

