The earliest game published in this line comes from 1899, and was played against leading American chess master [[w:Harry Nelson Pillsbury|Harry Nelson Pillsbury]] in an simultaneous exhibition. The game was reported in the ''Brooklyn Daily Eagle'' which gave the line the name '''Chicago gambit''' (after where the simul was played) as well as the '''Irish''' and '''Razzle Dazzle gambit'''.<ref>''Brooklyn Daily Eagle'', 26 January 1899. p. 12. As reproduced in {{Cite web |title=Unusual Chess Openings |url=https://www.chesshistory.com/winter/extra/unusualchessopenings.html |last=Winter |first=Edward |date=2026-01-10 |access-date=2026-08-23 |website=Chess Notes}}</ref><ref>[https://www.chessgames.com/perl/chessgame?gid=1288574 Phillips v Pillsbury, 1899. Chessgames.com]</ref>

Some online opening books give 3. Nxe5?? (or the continuation 3...Nxe5 4. d4) the name "'''Schulze-Müller gambit'''", which is also used for the similar line [[Chess Opening Theory/1. e4/1...e5/2. Nf3/2...Nc6/3. Nc3/3...Nf6/4. Nxe5|3. Nc3 Nf6 4. Nxe5?]].<ref>{{Cite web |title=A BREEZE IN THE SLEEPY 4-KNIGHT'S GAME |url=https://timkr.home.xs4all.nl/tour/breeze.htm |last=Krabbe |first=Tim |date=2000 |access-date=2026-08-23}}</ref> 

The name '''Irish gambit''' comes from a piece of 19th century ethnic humour. A joke article in the ''Columbia Chess Chronicle'', 1887, relates the misadventures of "Dennis O'Flaherty". O'Flaherty, inspired by Irish chess masters [[w:Alexander McDonnell (chess player)|Alexander MacDonnell]] and "La Borden Naigh" ([[w:Louis-Charles Mahé de La Bourdonnais|La Bourdonnais]], in fact French of course), takes up chess and plays 1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Nxe5?? admitting "I did not see the pawn was protected." A full scoresheet is not included, but the game ends when he misses mate in one and plays a check, to which Black's only legal move is to checkmate their opponent (see diagram).<ref>{{Cite web |title=Unusual Chess Openings |url=https://www.chesshistory.com/winter/extra/unusualchessopenings.html |last=Winter |first=Edward |date=2026-01-10 |access-date=2026-08-23 |website=Chess Notes}}</ref><ref>{{Cite journal |title=The Irish Gambit |journal=Columbia Chess Chronicle |date=1887-07-16 |url=https://books.google.co.uk/books?id=O5URwISX9HIC&newbks=1&newbks_redir=0&pg=PP39&redir_esc=y#v=onepage&q&f=false |volume=1 |issue=3 |pages=17-18}}</ref>

The other "Irish gambit", [[Chess Opening Theory/1. d4/1...d5/2. Nc3/2...c5|1. d4 d5 2. Nc3 c5]], where Black plays a Chigorin defence with colours reversed, is much more reputable and unrelated to this one. 

