== 7. c3 ==

White's c-pawn supports playing d4, which would cut off Black's bishop's attack on f2 and, White hopes, end the threat of ...Nxf2.  

The trickiest move is [[/7...Ng4|'''7...Ng4!?''']] anyway, where after 8. d4 Qh4 Black leaves their bishop hanging, but White can't take it because of 9. dxc5? Qxf2+ 10. Kd2 Bf5! 11. exf5?? O-O-O+, winning the queen. Instead, White must start with 9. g3 Qf6 (threatens ...Qxf2+ again) and the two lines are 10. f3 or 10. Bf3. 

[[/7...Bb6|'''7...Bd6?!''']] sets a different trap. It prevents d4 from coming with tempo, so if White plays it anyway they blunder a pawn: 8. d4? Nxe4 9. O-O and though White is still ahead, their edge has been cut considerably. Instead, White needs to prepare d4 by first defending the e-pawn, 8. Nd2, or in the order 8. a4 (threatening a5 and trapping the bishop) a5 9. Bg5 (pinning the knight) Qd6 10. Nd2.

