== 6...Nxe4! ==

Black springs into action and offers their queen as sacrifice. Accepting it is poisonous for White, in fact Black has devastating continuations for all of White's moves. 

If [[/7. Bxd8|'''7. Bxd8??''']], capturing the queen, Black has forced checkmate: 7...Bxf2+ 8. Ke2 Bg4#.

Unfortunately no other move for White is great either.

* [[/7. dxe4|'''7. dxe4??''']], taking the knight, lets Black win White's queen. 7. dxe4?? opens the d file, and Black can force White's king away from protecting d1 to snap up their queen. 7...Bxf2+ 8. Ke2 Bg4+ 9. Kxf2 Qxd1 {{Chess/not|--}}. 

* [[/7. Qe2|'''7. Qe2''']] to pin the knight allows 7...Qxg5. White's best move is 8. f4: but Black can just trade down and go into an endgame up a minor piece. (8...Bf2+ 9. Kd1 Qg4 10. Nd2 Qxe2+ 11. Kxe2 Nxd2 12. Kxf2 Nxf1{{Chess/not|---}}). 
: If 8. Qxe4+ Kd8 White must deal with both the threat of Re8, pinning White's queen to their king, and of Qc1+, infiltrating White's back rank (e.g. 9. Be2 Qc1+ 10. Bd1 Re8!). 
: If 8. dxe4?? Qc1+ 9. Qd1 Bxf2+ 10. Kxf2 (Ke2 11. Qe3#) Qxd1{{Chess/not|---}}. 

* Therefore White's best seventh move is to prevent Bxf2+ by retreating their bishop: [[/7. Be3|'''7. Be3''']], giving up thoughts of winning Black's queen. 

: However, Black is winning even here, as after trading 7...Bxe3 8. fxe3 White's kingside is too weak. 8...Qh4+ 9. g3 Nxg3 10. hxg3 Qxh1{{Chess/not|--}} and Black is up a rook.

