= Muzio Gambit =
White recaptures with their queen and prepares to start a big attack as compensation for the lost knight. White attacks the f4 pawn and this is really a major threat; for example, 6...Nc6?, the so-called Holloway Defence, runs into trouble after 7. Qxf4! Qe7 (if 7...Nh6??, 8. Bxf7+! Ke7 9. Qf6# ends the game) 8. Bxf7+! Kd8 9. Nc3! (threatening Nd5). Black has two main options:

* [[Chess Opening Theory/1. e4/1...e5/2. f4/2...exf4/3. Nf3/3...g5/4. Bc4/4...g4/5. O-O/5...gxf3/6. Qxf3/6...Qf6|'''6...Qf6''']], the Sarratt Defence, is almost always played, defending the attacked f4 pawn while adding another defender to the weak f7 square and challenging White to prove sufficient compensation for the material loss.
* '''[[Chess Opening Theory/1. e4/1...e5/2. f4/2...exf4/3. Nf3/3...g5/4. Bc4/4...g4/5. O-O/5...gxf3/6. Qxf3/6...Qe7|6...Qe7]]''', the From's Defence, has an interesting idea. If White gets greedy and captures the seemingly free f4 pawn, 7...Qc5+! picks up the bishop on c4.{{Chess Opening Theory/Footer}}
{{BookCat}}

{{Chess Opening Theory/Position|Muzio Gambit, Sarratt Defence}}
= Muzio Gambit, Sarratt Defence =
Black defends their f4 pawn and f7 pawn while developing the queen and blocking the f-file. This is the most testing reply to the Muzio Gambit as it forces White to prove sufficient compensation for the knight sacrifice. Black also carries the threat of 7...Qd4+ winning the bishop on c4. White must not let their attack fizzle out; for example, the most common move at club level is 7. c3, but this is too slow. There are other, better options available for White.

* [[Chess Opening Theory/1. e4/1...e5/2. f4/2...exf4/3. Nf3/3...g5/4. Bc4/4...g4/5. O-O/5...gxf3/6. Qxf3/6...Qf6/7. d3|'''7. d3''']] is the soundest move for White in this position, but some players consider it slow. It allows the c1 bishop to view the f4 pawn, thus adding another attacker to it.
* [[Chess Opening Theory/1. e4/1...e5/2. f4/2...exf4/3. Nf3/3...g5/4. Bc4/4...g4/5. O-O/5...gxf3/6. Qxf3/6...Qf6/7. e5|'''7. e5''']] often leads to chaotic play. More common that 7. d3 due to its aggressive style, this often leads to the Double Muzio Gambit, 7...Qxe5 8. Bxf7+, where White sacrifices their bishop as well.
* [[Chess Opening Theory/1. e4/1...e5/2. f4/2...exf4/3. Nf3/3...g5/4. Bc4/4...g4/5. O-O/5...gxf3/6. Qxf3/6...Qf6/7. Nc3|'''7. Nc3?''']], the Bello Gambit, tries to distract the queen from defending the f4 pawn by tempting 7...Qd4+, but this simply does not work. After 8. Kh1 Qxc4, White can try either 9. Qxf4 or 9. Nd5 (the ''Baldwin Gambit''), but they can be easily diffused: 9. Qxf4 Bd6-+ or 9. Nd5 d6-+ and Black is up two pieces.
* [[Chess Opening Theory/1. e4/1...e5/2. f4/2...exf4/3. Nf3/3...g5/4. Bc4/4...g4/5. O-O/5...gxf3/6. Qxf3/6...Qf6/7. b3|'''7. b3!?''']] is an extremely rare move which sets a somewhat dastardly trap; 7...Qxa1 8. Nc3 Bg7?? 9. Qxf4! and White is almost completely winning due to the undefendable f7 square and the queen on a1 stuck out of play, not able to do anything.
{{Chess Opening Theory/Footer}}
{{BookCat}}

{{Chess  diagram|tright|'''Kieseritzky Gambit,  Berlin Defence'''|rd|nd|bd|qd|kd|bd||rd|pd|pd|pd|||pd||pd||||||nd||||||pd|nl||||||bl||pl|pd|pd|pl|||||||||pl|pl|pl|pl|||pl|pl|rl|nl|bl|ql|kl|||rl|'''Common moves:'''<br> [[/7. exd5|7. exd5 - only move]]|=}}
White's c4-bishop and e4-pawn are forked by the Black pawn on d5. [[Chess Opening Theory/1. e4/1...e5/2. f4/2...exf4/3. Nf3/3...g5/4. h4/4...g4/5. Ne5/5...Nf6/6. Bc4/6...d5/7. exd5|'''7. exd5''']] is necessary to remove the threat. 

7. Bxd5 would be a positional disaster for White. It would give Black a powerful centralised queen after 7...Nxd5 8.exd5 Qxd5, it would bring White no closer to recovering the pawn, and it would give up the bishop pair for no reason. In the analogous position after 7.exd5, 7...Nxd5 would not oblige White to capture the knight.{{wikipedia|King's Gambit}}{{Chess Opening  Theory/Footer}}

{{Chess  diagram|tright|'''Kieseritzky Gambit'''|rd|nd|bd|qd|kd|bd|nd|rd|pd|pd|pd|||pd||pd||||pd|||||||||||||||||pl|pd|nl|pl|||||||||pl|pl|pl|pl|||pl||rl|nl|bl|ql|kl|bl||rl|'''Common moves:'''<br> [[/6...Nf6|6...Nf6 - Main Line]]<br>  [[/6...Be7|6...Be7 - Staunton Variation]]|=}}

[[Chess Opening Theory/1. e4/1...e5/2. f4/2...exf4/3. Nf3/3...g5/4. h4/4...g4/5. Ne5/5...d6/6. Nxg4/6...Nf6|'''6...Nf6''']] is the usual move. Black aims to come out with a lead in development after an exchange of knights on f6, that will compensate for her suspect kingside pawn structure.

[[Chess Opening Theory/1. e4/1...e5/2. f4/2...exf4/3. Nf3/3...g5/4. h4/4...g4/5. Ne5/5...d6/6. Nxg4/6...Be7|'''6...Be7''']] is a move that lay gathering dust on a shelf for a century and a half, between Howard Staunton losing a famous game with it against Baron Tassilo von Heydebrand und der Lasa in 1853 and the computers rediscovering it in the 1990s. It's had a very positive score from a small sample size. Black threatens to take White's pawn on h4, because taking pawns means you have more of them than your opponent does.{{wikipedia|King's Gambit}}{{Chess Opening  Theory/Footer}}

