== 7...Qc7 · Poisoned pawn variation ==
Black lets White take the pawn on g7, but will break apart White's queenside in return. If White chooses to accept Black's pawn sacrifice, incredibly complex positions arise where general chess principles lose all meaning. 

After '''[[/8. Qxg7/]]''', the main line, complications arise. The destruction of Black's kingside forces them to eventually castle long, where White has the open b-file to be used in an attack. Moreover, if all pieces are traded and an endgame arises, White will have good chances of promoting the passed h-pawn. Black will have to respond aggressively.

Less common is '''[[/8. Bd3/]]''', declining the pawn for now and preparing to castle short.

=== History ===
This variation was first played in 1945 between [[w:Vasily Panov|Vasily Panov]] and [[w:Viacheslav Ragozin|Viacheslav Ragozin]].<ref>{{cite web
|url=https://www.chessgames.com/perl/chessgame?gid=1116528
|website=www.chessgames.com
|title=Panov - Ragozin, Moscow, 1945
|access-date=10 February 2026
}}</ref> The game followed what is still considered to be the main line up to move 13, where Panov played 13. Be3. This can be attributed to the [[../7...cxd4|7...cxd4]] move order having been employed a decade prior already.<ref>https://www.chessgames.com/perl/chess.pl?node=86237</ref><ref>{{cite web
|url=https://www.chessgames.com/perl/chessgame?gid=1981455
|website=www.chessgames.com
|title=Neishtadt - Estrin, Moscow, 1938
|access-date=10 February 2026
}}
</ref>

