== 4. Qg4 · Nimzowitsch attack ==
{{Chess/sideline}}
While this move may look imposing, it poses no threats. Usually in the French defence, this move is played once Black has moved their dark-squared bishop, which would threaten the g-pawn. Despite the lack of direct threats, White can quickly build up their forces on the kingside, playing for a quick attack. For now, the queen on g4 also prevents Black from moving their dark-squared bishop. Despite its imposing nature, this move is considered unsound, although it might be an interesting surprise weapon if prepared well.<ref>{{cite web
|url=https://www.chesspublishing.com/french/2001-06-01#adv
|website=chesspublishing.com
|title=GM Neil McDonald on Advance, Winawer Mainline 7 Q-0 Winawer Mainline 'Declined' 5...Ba5 Tarrasch 3...Nf6 Tarrasch 3...c5 4 exd5 Qxd5 Tarrasch 3...Be7 Classical 4 Bg5 dxe4 5 Nxe4 Nbd7 Classical 4 e5
}}</ref>

Because White doesn't defend their d-pawn, Black can simply take it with '''4...cxd4'''. 5. Qxd4? would be unwise, since this loses many valuable tempi in the opening. White can't afford to be materialistic, so they should sacrifice the pawn and continue development with 5. Nf3 Nc6 6. Bd3 Qc7?!. Modern engines give the position after 6...Qc7 as slightly better for Black (-0.5). However, after 6...h5, displacing the queen, evaluation clearly prefers Black (-1.0). This is also reflected in the lichess database, where Black scores best with 6...h5, despite the complicated position.<ref>https://lichess.org/analysis/pgn/1.+e4+e6+2.+d4+d5+3.+e5+c5+4.Qg4+cxd4+5.Nf3+Nc6+6.Bd3</ref>

'''4...Nc6''' is an alternative, which can transpose to the previous line after 5. Nf3 cxd4. An independent line arises after 5. Nf3 Qa5+ 6. Bd2 which may also pose some problems for White.

=== History ===
The opening is named after [[w:Aron Nimzowitsch|Aron Nimzowitsch]], who played this move for the first time in 1922 against Arthur Hakansson.<ref>{{cite web
|website=chessgames.com
|url=https://www.chessgames.com/perl/chessgame?gid=1102392
|title=Nimzowitsch - Hakansson, 1922
}}</ref>
{{Quote|text=[My] innovation, played here for the first time. As will be seen, the basis of the idea is overprotection.|sign=Aron Nimzowitsch<ref>{{cite book
|title=Die Praxis meines Systems
|author=Aron Nimzowitsch
|page=190
|isbn=0713450584
}}</ref>}}
In the game, Nimzowitsch played by overprotecting the e5-pawn to keep Black's position cramped. This was made easier because after 4...cxd4 5. Nf3 Nc6 6. Bd3 Black played the dubious 6...f5? due to which the e5-pawn can never be attacked by any of Black's pawns.

