== 5...Bd7 · Wade variation ==
Since the light-squared bishop is considered a 'problem piece' in the French defence, Black tries to trade it for White's bishop. The downside of this plan is that it will cost Black a few tempi, both for moving the bishop and for repositioning the queen. White has a variety of responses.

'''6. a3''' is the most popular. Similar to the main line, this prepares a queenside expansion. After 6...Bb5 White is at another crossroads: they can try to open up the centre with 7. c4!? Bxc4 8. Bxc4 dxc4 d5 or they can continue expanding on the queenside while trying to gain some tempo on Black's queen with 7. b4 cxd4 (7...Bxf1? 8. dxc5 Qa6 9. Rxf1{{Chess/not|++}}) 8. Bxb5+ Qxb5 9. cxd4 Nd7 10. Nc3 Qc6. If Black gets cold feet, they can transpose back to the mainline with 6...Nc6.

