'''6. Be2''' allows White to trade the bishop on slightly more favourable terms, since they can force Black to capture and then recapture with their queen. After 6...Bb5 there is once again the resource of 7. c4 to open up the centre, which gets into the same position as 6. a3 Bb5 7. c4, but with the pawn still on a2. The trade can also be prevented while expanding on the queenside after 6...Bb5 7. dxc5 Bxc5 8. b4!? Bxf2+ 9. Kf1 Bc6 (9...Bxe2+?? 10. Qxe2 traps the bishop) 10. a4 a6.

'''6. Bd3''' is the third most played option. The idea is similar to 6. Be2, where White tries to get Black to take the bishop on White's terms. Once again, White has the idea of 6...Bb5 7. dxc5 Bxc5 8. b4, but now Black can trade light-squared bishops with 8...Bxd3 9. Qxd3. 

