== 2. d4 ==
Since Black has not contested the centre yet, White takes more space. They are ready to develop their minor pieces. 

=== Control the centre with pieces ===

Black would like to focus on development and attack the centre from a distance. ...Bg7 and ...d6 is the immediate plan, usually played in the order [[/2...Bg7|'''2...Bg7''']] and 3...d6, though either way is possible. Black usually accepts a small centre of their own, with pawns on d6 and e6. 

The role of '''...d6''' is to control the e5 square and prevent White from pushing a pawn there. This means that the bishop (once fianchettoed) will be able to surveil the centre, and Black will eventually be able to play ...Nf6 without facing e5. 

[[/2...Nf6|'''2...Nf6!?''']] immediately is called the '''Norwegian defence'''. Without the protection a pawn on d6, the knight may be kicked with 3. e5!. This is usually played with the idea of a gambit 3...Nh5 4. Be2 d6 allowing 5. Bxh5 gxh5 6. Qxh5. 

=== Control the centre with pawns !? ===

Having allowed White to establish their big two-pawn centre, it is difficult now for Black to contest it with ...e5 or ...d5. Black may prepare ...d5 with [[/2...c6|'''2...c6''']]. 3. Nc3 d5 is a transposition into the Gurgenidze system of the Caro-Kann defence. 

'''3...d6''' prevents White from pushing a pawn to e5 but it is insufficient to support Black playing ...e5 themself. 3. Nc3 e5? 4. dxe5 dxe5 5. Qxd8+ Kxd8 {{chess/not|++}} and Black has a strictly worse Philidor. 

Black may instead consider a flank attack on the pawn centre. 

[[/2...f5|'''2...f5?''']], the '''fianchetto gambit''' is bad. Black offers a pawn and weakens their kingside to deflect White's e-pawn, intending 3. exf5 d5 (not 3...gxf5?? 4. Qh5#). 

Attacking on the other flank is usually played in the order 2...Bg7 3. Nc3 c5!?, and is known as the '''pterodactyl defence'''. 

